/** \addtogroup area_mfset */
/** \defgroup theory_mfset Theory of merge-find sets
 \ingroup area_mfset

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/mfset` realize the MFSET of section
 10.2 of the book, the union of disjoint sets, on lists (section 10.2.1) and
 on a forest of parents (section 10.2.2) with the union by rank and the
 compression of the paths (sections 10.2.3 and 10.2.4), and, beyond the
 book, two engineered forests (one in a single vector of integers, with the
 union by size and the halving of the paths, and the algorithm of Rem with
 splicing) and a forest whose merges can be undone, with the connectivity of
 a graph whose edges come and go. This page collects the definitions and the
 notation that the headers share (see \ref theory_mfset_notation), the
 realizations with the costs of their operations (see
 \ref theory_mfset_repr), and the arguments behind the bounds of the
 heuristics (see \ref theory_mfset_union and \ref theory_mfset_paths) and
 of the undo (see \ref theory_mfset_rollback). The pseudocode of each
 operation is on the page of its header, in the comment of its function,
 together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_mfset_notation Definitions and notation

 An MFSET (merge-find set) is a partition of the elements
 \f$ \{ 0 , \ldots , n - 1 \} \f$ into disjoint <em>components</em>, which
 starts with the \f$ n \f$ singletons \f$ \{ 0 \} , \ldots , \{ n - 1 \} \f$
 and changes by two operations: \f$ \mathrm{find}( x ) \f$ gives the
 <em>representative</em> of the component of \f$ x \f$, an element of it that
 stays the same until the component changes, so that \f$ x \f$ and
 \f$ y \f$ are in the same component if and only if
 \f$ \mathrm{find}( x ) = \mathrm{find}( y ) \f$; and
 \f$ \mathrm{merge}( x , y ) \f$ replaces the components of \f$ x \f$ and of
 \f$ y \f$ with their union. Components are never split. The MFSET follows
 the connected components of a graph whose edges arrive one at a time, i.e.,
 the cycles that the algorithm of Kruskal must refuse (see
 \ref theory_graph_mst), and the classes of an equivalence relation given by
 its pairs.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ m \f$</td><td>the number of elements, and the
     number of operations of a sequence of finds and merges</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ p[ x ] \f$</td><td>the parent of \f$ x \f$ in the forest,
     \f$ p[ x ] = x \f$ (or a negative number in <tt>UnionFind</tt>) at a
     root, the representative</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{rank}[ r ] \f$</td><td>the rank of the root
     \f$ r \f$, an upper bound to the height of its tree</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{size}[ r ] \f$</td><td>the number of nodes of the tree
     of the root \f$ r \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha( m , n ) \f$</td><td>the inverse of the function of
     Ackermann, at most 4 for any \f$ m \f$ and \f$ n \f$ that fit in a
     computer</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_mfset_repr Realizations and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the MFSET and the cost of their operations
          (section 10.2 of the book), for \f$ m \ge n \f$ operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Representation</th>
     <th><tt>find()</tt></th><th><tt>merge()</tt></th>
     <th>\f$ m \f$ operations</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ListMFSet</tt></td><td>a list per component, each element
     with the head of its list</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$, the shorter list relabeled</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MFSet</tt>, union <tt>NONE</tt>, no compression</td>
     <td>a forest of parents</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>that of two
     finds</td><td>\f$ O( m n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MFSet</tt>, union <tt>RANK</tt> or <tt>SIZE</tt></td>
     <td>as above</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>that of two finds</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MFSet</tt> by rank or size with compression,
     <tt>UnionFind</tt></td><td>as above, or one vector with the sizes at
     the roots</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>that of two finds</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RemUnionFind</tt></td><td>a forest with
     \f$ p[ x ] \ge x \f$, linked by index</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>one interleaved climb</td><td>\f$ O( m \log_{1 + m / n} n ) \f$,
     \f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$ expected on a random
     numbering</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RollbackUnionFind</tt></td><td>a forest by rank, no
     compression, and a stack of the merges</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$
     in the worst case</td><td>that of two finds; <tt>undo()</tt> in
     \f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( m \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 On lists, `find()` reads the head that each element keeps, and `merge()`
 appends one list to the other and relabels the elements of the appended
 one; on a forest, `find()` climbs from \f$ x \f$ to the root of its tree,
 and `merge()` makes one root a child of the other, after the two finds.
 The two realizations trade one cost for the other, and the heuristics
 below bring the forest to a cost per operation that is constant in
 practice.

 \section theory_mfset_union Unions by size and by rank

 <em>Lemma 1 (the relabeling of the shorter list, Hopcroft and Ullman).</em>
 If `merge()` relabels the elements of the shorter list, an element is
 relabeled at most \f$ \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor \f$ times, and any sequence of
 \f$ m \f$ operations costs \f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$.

 Indeed, an element is relabeled only when its list is the shorter of the
 two, and then the list it lands in is at least twice as long as its old
 one; a list has at most \f$ n \f$ elements, so that this happens at most
 \f$ \log_2 n \f$ times per element. The finds cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ each.

 On a forest with no heuristic, \f$ n - 1 \f$ merges may build a path of
 \f$ n \f$ nodes, whose deepest node costs \f$ n - 1 \f$ steps to every
 find. The union by rank keeps for each root \f$ r \f$ a rank, 0 for a
 singleton, and puts the root of lower rank under the other one, the rank
 growing by 1 only when the two ranks are equal; the union by size puts the
 tree with fewer nodes under the other one.

 <em>Lemma 2 (Theorem 10.1 of the book).</em> With the union by rank, a tree
 whose root has rank \f$ r \f$ has at least \f$ 2^r \f$ nodes and height at
 most \f$ r \f$; hence \f$ r \le \log_2 n \f$, and `find()` and `merge()`
 take \f$ O( \log n ) \f$.

 Indeed, by induction on the merges: a singleton has rank 0 and one node;
 a merge of two roots of different ranks keeps the rank and the height of
 the higher tree, and adds nodes to it; a merge of two roots of equal rank
 \f$ r \f$ gives rank \f$ r + 1 \f$ and height at most \f$ r + 1 \f$ to a
 tree of at least \f$ 2^r + 2^r = 2^{r+1} \f$ nodes. With the union by size
 the same bound holds by the argument of Lemma 1: a node goes one level
 down only when its tree joins one at least as large, i.e., when the tree
 that holds it at least doubles, so that its depth is at most
 \f$ \log_2 \mathit{size}[ r ] \f$.

 \section theory_mfset_paths Compression, halving and splicing of the paths

 A find that has climbed from \f$ x \f$ to its root \f$ r \f$ may make each
 node of the path a child of \f$ r \f$ (the <em>compression</em> of section
 10.2.4), in a second pass, so that the next finds from those nodes cost one
 step. The <em>halving</em> makes each node of the path a child of its
 grandparent instead, in the same pass that climbs, and the
 <em>splicing</em> of Rem replaces a parent with a larger parent of the
 other element while two climbs go on together; both shorten every path
 they walk by about half.

 <em>Theorem 3 (Tarjan, Tarjan and van Leeuwen).</em> With the union by rank
 or by size, and the compression or the halving of the paths, a sequence of
 \f$ m \ge n \f$ operations on \f$ n \f$ elements costs
 \f[
   O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) , \tag{1}
 \f]
 and no algorithm on a forest of pointers does better in the worst case.

 The argument, which the book sketches and Tarjan and van Leeuwen give in
 full, charges each step of a find either to the operation, at most a
 constant number of steps per operation, or to the node, whose parent then
 changes to one of much larger rank; since the ranks along a path increase,
 and a node of rank \f$ r \f$ is one of at most \f$ n / 2^r \f$, the charges
 to the nodes sum to \f$ O( m \, \alpha( m , n ) ) \f$ over the levels of
 the inverse of the function of Ackermann. The bound (1) is
 \f$ O( m ) \f$ in practice, since \f$ \alpha \f$ is at most 4 for every
 input that fits in a computer; a single find may still cost
 \f$ \Theta( \log n ) \f$.

 The engineered forests of the area keep (1) and save memory and passes.
 `UnionFind` keeps the parents and, at each root, minus the size of its tree
 in one vector of integers, so that a find touches one array, and halves
 the paths in the single pass of the climb. `RemUnionFind` replaces the
 union by rank with the <em>link by index</em>: every parent is at least its
 child, \f$ p[ x ] \ge x \f$, so that a path increases strictly to its root,
 and `merge()` climbs from \f$ x \f$ and \f$ y \f$ together, always from the
 node of smaller parent, which, if it is a root, is linked under the parent
 of the other node; otherwise its parent is replaced by the larger parent of
 the other one, which keeps \f$ p[ x ] \ge x \f$. The climb stops as soon as
 the two components are joined, or the two nodes reach the same parent,
 often well below the roots. The link by index alone gives the worst-case
 bound \f$ O( m \log_{1 + m / n} n ) \f$ (Tarjan and van Leeuwen), and the
 bound (1) in expectation when the elements are numbered in a random order
 (Goel, Khanna, Larkin and Tarjan); the experiments of Patwary, Blair and
 Manne found it among the fastest in practice.

 \section theory_mfset_rollback Undo of the merges and offline connectivity

 Beyond the book, `RollbackUnionFind` undoes its merges in the reverse of
 their order: each `merge()` changes at most two words, the parent of the
 root that it hangs and the rank of the other root, and pushes them on a
 stack, from which `undo()` puts them back in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$;
 `rollback( t )` undoes the merges back to the checkpoint \f$ t \f$, the
 number of merges done when `checkpoint()` gave it. The forest keeps the
 union by rank and gives up any change of the paths: a compression would
 change \f$ \Theta( \log n ) \f$ parents per find, all to be recorded, and
 its amortized bound (1) does not survive the undo, since a sequence that
 rolls back the merges and does them again can make the same long find pay
 each time. With no compression, Lemma 2 bounds every find by
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ in the worst case, not only amortized, and an undo
 restores exactly the forest before the merge, heights and ranks included;
 Westbrook and Tarjan show that, with backtracking, this is as good as any
 of the usual compressions, \f$ \Theta( \log n ) \f$ per operation in the
 worst case of a sequence.

 The undo makes it possible to answer offline the questions of connectivity
 on a graph whose edges come and go (`offlineConnectivity()`), when the
 whole sequence of \f$ k \f$ insertions, deletions and questions is known in
 advance. Each edge lives on an interval of times \f$ [ s , e ) \f$, from its
 insertion to its deletion (or to the end), and the \f$ q \f$ questions are
 the leaves of a segment tree, whose node of range \f$ [ a , b ) \f$ holds
 the list \f$ L \f$ of the edges that live on all of \f$ [ a , b ) \f$ and
 not on all of the range of its parent. A visit in depth of the segment tree
 merges the edges of \f$ L \f$ when it enters a node and rolls them back when
 it leaves it.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the segment tree over the times).</em> An interval of times
 is held by at most \f$ 2 \lceil \log_2 q \rceil \f$ nodes of the segment
 tree, and when the visit reaches the leaf of a question the forest holds
 exactly the edges alive at its time; hence the answers are those of a
 union-find built again at each question, and the time is
 \f[
   O( k \log q \log n ) = O( k \log k \log n ) . \tag{2}
 \f]

 Indeed, at each level of the segment tree at most two nodes hold part of
 the interval without holding all of it, as in the decomposition of a range
 by a segment tree, so that the interval is split into at most two nodes
 per level. The edges merged at the leaf are those of its ancestors, i.e.,
 those whose interval holds a range around the leaf, which are the edges
 alive at its time, and the rollback when leaving a node restores the
 forest of its parent. Each edge is merged and undone \f$ O( \log q ) \f$
 times, at \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ each by Lemma 2, which gives (2), with
 \f$ O( k \log k ) \f$ space for the lists. The divide and conquer on the
 times is the one of Eppstein for the dynamic minimum spanning tree; with
 the contraction of the graph at each level (Eppstein, and Peng, Sandlund
 and Sleator) the bound falls to \f$ O( k \log n ) \f$, at the price of a
 far longer code, which the rollback avoids.

